A||N||8||FieroSTETZ||powermac 8500 - how do i use the capture things?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||10-08-2001||11:46 PM||||it has a built in capture system, and video out. how do i make use of these? - want to do more video editing and do it on my mac- help me! haha<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/Animation1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||beer_stud_21||10-09-2001||09:10 PM||||I'm writing this on a PM 8500.  If you want to use the video in/out ports for video editing you are going to need Adobe Premier or Strata Studio.  Both do analog video editing.  Depending on how serious about this stuff you are you will need a BIG/FAST hard drive to capture to.  If you are just trying to capture small quicktime videos to your computer to post on a website or someting you can use "Apple Video Player" that is free from apple.  It sould already be installed on your computer.  If you want to do anything close to professional level you should get a mac with firewire ports (or a firewire card for your 8500) and a firewire equipped camera.  Then use either iMovie (low end-free with new apple computers) or Final Cut Pro which is a professional level editing application.  It is used quite a bit for broadcast tv etc.  Just be sure you do your research if you go with firewire.  I have heard of some people having problems capturing DV video to older macs with firewire cards.   Hope I answered some of your questions.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroSTETZ||10-10-2001||02:48 AM||||have you modified your powermac 8500 at all? was thinking about an upgrade (g3 probably) i have a firewire card and a firewire harddrive, will probably capture to this. have final cut pro 2, it just does not run well on the 8500- i cant believe the video quality compared to the aftermarket capture card quality, this is great! thanks for the info! BTW, found a free utility called hacktv, works great!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.shawnrocks.com/Animation1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||10-10-2001||02:24 PM||||Yeah, I've modified my 8500.  Turbomax IDE card w/30 gig maxtor, USB PCI, Yamaha SCSI cd burner, 275mhz railgun G3 card overclocked to 350mhz.  Also had a voodoo3 in here up until about a week ago.  How much ram do you have?  FPM dimms are cheaper than they have ever been right now. If you are planning on keeping your system and doing video editing with it you really should have at least 256mb ram I would say.  I am going to be buying a new(er) system in a few weeks.  When I do that I would consider selling you my G3 card out of this one.  As well as an essentually brand new internal  scsi zip 100 drive.<P>-marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||10-11-2001||01:37 PM||||hey, neta- i have 176 mb of ram, cant really find anywhere to buy the ram- where do you find it? i am building a system for my girlfriend, shes in school doing aftereffects and photoshop, and has a amd k6-2/400 w/ 64 mb of ram, not really a primo system for the work. right now the computer has a 4gb scsi hd (adding firewire if it will take it, have a bunch of firewire harddrives) a 2x scsi burner (need to get a compliant ide controller the mac can boot off of.) hey, the zip and the g3 card would be awesome, let me know when they are available. also, i think i got ahold of a shrink wrapped 10.1 upgrade for ya, need to figure out shipping (might just take it to work and have them send it :P ) trying to figure out what parts from a umax (performa 6360 clone) and performa 6360 i can use on this.. need any parts from either of those?||||reg||1
Z||000005||kslish||10-12-2001||12:54 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.velocityupgrades.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.velocityupgrades.com/</A> <P>128meg FPM or EDO DIMMS for $25.99<P>Rumor has it that they are really system pulls (in other words used) but I've had good success with them and they warrant the memory.<P>I'm using them in my G3 upgraded UMAX S900 (PowerMac 9500 clone) that I put together out of spare parts and am running OS X on (I'm too cheap to shell out the cash for a real G3 or G4 and I have a lot of SCSI stuff).  Can't even get close to that price on memory for these older Macs anywhere else.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000006||beer_stud_21||12-03-2001||06:30 PM||||Hey Kslish,<P>When you installed OS X did you need to use an Apple rommed CD drive?  I'm tring to install OS X on a beige G3 and have been able to install 10.0.3 (and boot to that cd) but the 10.1 upgrade cd wont boot from my 4x scsi drive.<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000007||Howard_Sacks||12-03-2001||11:57 PM||||YOU GUYS ARE OLD SCHOOL!<P>FieroSTETZ: you should be able to capture with "Apple Video Player" at acceptable frame rates (at least I was with my 7600, although I think we were using a freeware util from shareware.com for the highest quality import), but if you are doing any sort of editing, get a firewire equiped mac.  Even a $500 used DV iMac will do good(although if you're encoding for the web, G4's are worth the money). Trust me it is not worth the headaches of dealing with 3rd party firewire cards and video capturing.<P>Kslish: s900 is my all time favorite machine! (Btw, 9500's SUCK!) I had a s900 pimped out with a Powerlogix G3 375 OC'd to 400 with 1mb of cache, 272mg of ram, initio miles u2 scsi, atlas hds, firewire, usb, voodoo3, ati rage vr, orange pc(I have 2 unused if any of you guys want pc compatibility cheap, will trade for fiero parts). I loved that machine.<P>I now have a yikes! G4 with a dual usb iBook. They just "work" so it's not as much fun.||||reg||1
Z||000008||beer_stud_21||12-04-2001||06:55 AM||||Nothin wrong with old school!  I just successfully installed OS 10.1 on this Beige G3 and am lovin it!  We bought my mom a dual USB iBook and it's great.  I love the screen.    I've also had a chance to use a TiBook which was <B>very</B> nice!<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
